    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let me try to simplify it.\n\nIn classical correlation, the correlation between two events is a statistical probability. If you roll two dice, the probability of them matching is 1 in 36, but it's not a guaranteed outcome. The correlation is just a coincidence.\n\nIn entanglement, the correlation is not just statistical, but a fundamental property of the particles themselves. When two particles are entangled, their properties are connected in a way that can't be explained by classical probability. Measuring one particle instantly affects the state of the other, regardless of the distance between them.\n\nThink of it like a coin flip. In classical correlation, flipping one coin doesn't affect the outcome of the other coin. But in entanglement, flipping one coin is like flipping a \"goblin switch\" - it instantly affects the state of the other coin, even if they're on opposite sides of the universe.\n\nDoes that make more sense?",
      "source": "target",
